.PP
Plugins add commands of their own, \f[CR]mfd <plugin> ...\f[R]: see
notes, slack and github.
.PP
Closing a tab or canceling a page load can disconnect it while the
server answers.
\f[CR]mfd serve\f[R] closes that connection without a traceback;
\f[CR]\-v\f[R] logs the disconnect at debug level.
Other request errors still report their failures.
.SH PANELS
The page has \f[B]Act now\f[R] on top: the best next actions across
every tool, at most 7, no more than 3 from one plugin, one per PR or
ticket.
They\(aqre shown in a panel per plugin, so they flow across the page,
the panel with the best item first, and each is badged with its place
overall (\f[CR]#1\f[R], \f[CR]#2\f[R], ...); focus mode\(aqs Act now
goes through them in that order.
Each says what to do and why it\(aqs there (\(dqquick, closes APP\-12,
alice waiting\(dq), and anything else due on the same subject (\(dqalso:
Fix CI\(dq), with its buttons.
Finishing something counts most, then quick work, then someone waiting
(more the longer they\(aqve waited); important work and a nearing due
date add to that (\(dqcritical, alice waiting 3d, due in 2d\(dq);
blocked work ranks lower.
An item about a unit of work (moving a ticket, a work entry\(aqs todo)
joins the best item about something in it, to do after it, with its
buttons: \(dqReply: PR 330\(dq, then \(dqMove to In Review: APP\-12\(dq.
An item made of several parts, like an entry\(aqs todos, lists them with
their own buttons.
Each plugin nominates its own items; its page says which.
Each item has Snooze (for a while, by default until tomorrow, or until
it changes, picked beside it) and Pin (first until unpinned), which act
on all it shows: what else is due on its subject and what\(aqs joined to
it.
A snoozed item comes back once it changes (its title, badges or
details).
Opening an item\(aqs link, or pressing a plugin\(aqs button on it, marks
it opened: it\(aqs dimmed in its place, with when you opened it, until
it changes (or for a day at most).
Coming back to the tab refreshes the plugins of what you opened in the
last half hour, each fetching just that where it can (github a PR, git a
branch\(aqs repo); an item\(aqs ↻ button does the same for it at once.
.PP
Items with something to do have the same Snooze in their own panels.
An item snoozed, in Act now or its panel, leaves Act now and waits in
its panel, last and dimmed, with Unsnooze, until it changes or the
snooze ends; panel and group counts leave it out, and focus mode skips
it.
Pin is Act now\(aqs.
.PP
Below Act now is a collapsible group per enabled plugin holding its
panels.
Panels go in order of what kind of panel each is (its band): what I
planned (notes\(aq todos), what others ask of me (reviews, replies,
mail), my own work that needs me (failing checks, conflicts, feedback,
unpushed commits), what I\(aqm waiting on others for (reviews asked,
merges under way, nudges), housekeeping (PR labels, branches to clean
up, ticket statuses), what to pick up next, and what\(aqs on ice.
A plugin\(aqs panels in one band go in its own order, and groups by
their most urgent panel\(aqs band, then by name: notes, github, mail,
git, git_stack, slack, jira.
Drag a group\(aqs or a panel\(aqs title to move it (a panel within its
group; Act now stays first); the order is kept in the database, and
focus mode\(aqs list of panels follows it.
A group or panel new since then goes after the one it followed by
default.
Every panel orders its items by importance first (the priority set on an
item\(aqs subject, else what its plugin or who it\(aqs about gives it),
then by its own order.
Every item with an entity subject but what\(aqs on ice, including a todo
in the Notes panel or Focus mode, shows Important and Critical as one
group of buttons (❗️ / ‼️), highlighting the selected level.
To set something aside rather than raise it, Snooze or put it on ice.
For an item about several entities, the choice applies to its first
subject.
Once one is set, a Clear priority button goes with them.
Act now groups its Snooze and Pin choices too, with emoji icons.
Emoji buttons show their text in a hover title and an accessible name.
Reset layout, in the footer, goes back to the default order.
Only Act now starts expanded; each panel inside a group collapses too,
and starts open but when it\(aqs empty and has no field to fill in
(notes\(aq New todo).
Items can also nest under related items, with collapsible child lists.
The page remembers which groups, panels and child lists you opened, but
an empty panel with a field starts open whatever it remembers.
Each plugin\(aqs page lists its panels.
Dashboard action buttons update the page in place, keeping the scroll
position.
Without JavaScript, their normal form redirects still work.
.PP
A group\(aqs title bar also says when its plugin last refreshed (in
local time, with the date when it wasn\(aqt today),
\(dqrefreshing\&...\(dq while it does, or \(dqrefresh failed\(dq with
the reason on hover, and how many problems the plugin reports besides a
failed refresh (a lookup its token isn\(aqt allowed, say), each said
above its panels until it\(aqs gone.
Its Refresh button refreshes just that plugin, and then the plugins that
watch it (git_stack watches git) if what they read changed; the
header\(aqs Refresh refreshes them all.
The footer lists only plugins without a group, and their problems, and,
after the upgrade that made notes need a \f[CR][notes]\f[R] section,
that notes is off if it held something then and has had no section
since; adding \f[CR][notes]\f[R] turns it on and clears the line for
good.
While any plugin refreshes, the tab icon shows a dotted version of the
dashboard arrow, highlighting one dot at a time.
If the browser pauses the animation in a background tab, it still shows
a complete refresh icon.
The normal mark returns when the refresh ends.
.SH PEOPLE
The same person can be in several tools.
Core treats \f[CR]person\f[R] entities with the same email as one
person, whoever publishes them, so what a CRM says about someone counts
for their mail.
An address many people share (\f[CR]noreply\(at...\f[R]) joins no one.
.PP
An item about a person shows their contact type as a badge, and a Set
type button that sets it for that address or for everyone at its domain
(not for a domain anyone can have an address at, like gmail.com).
Once one is set, a Clear type button goes with it, asking first; one set
on a domain says so, as it clears everyone there.
A type I set beats one a tool gives, and one on an address beats one on
its domain.
Their type\(aqs importance (\f[CR][people] contact_types\f[R]) raises
the item in Act now and in its panel.
It never lowers what a plugin said, and a priority I set on the item
replaces it.
.SH TAGS
An item\(aqs tags come from its tool (a PR\(aqs or a ticket\(aqs labels:
each plugin\(aqs page says which), from a \f[CR]#tag\f[R] written in a
work entry\(aqs title or a todo (not in code), and from me: its Tag
field, on any item about something (a mail, a ticket, a branch), or
\f[CR]mfd tag\f[R].
Each is a \f[CR]#tag\f[R] badge, filters the page (below), and has an
Untag button, which takes it off whatever put it there.
One I put on goes for good; one from its tool stays off until the plugin
the item is from next refreshes, which puts it back if the tool still
has it; one from an entry\(aqs or a todo\(aqs text stays off until I Tag
it again.
A tag I put on stays on finished work (a merged PR, a done ticket); what
I put on or took off is forgotten once the item is gone (a deleted
entry, a ticked\-off todo, a mail once read).
.PP
A tag\(aqs words are separated by \f[CR]\-\f[R] or \f[CR]_\f[R], and it
needs a letter (\f[CR]#7\f[R] is a PR\(aqs number).
Text that names a tag, in a title or a description, however its words
are spaced, hyphenated or cased (\(dqGarbage day moved\(dq names
\f[CR]#garbage\-day\f[R]), points at it; a one\-word tag shorter than
three letters, or a tag only labels give (often a common word, like
\f[CR]bug\f[R]), is named only with its \f[CR]#\f[R].
Tags relate items without making them one piece of work: icing one, or
setting its priority, doesn\(aqt touch the others.
Core publishes each as a \f[CR]tag\f[R] entity (see entities.md); a
plugin reads which tags are on, or named by, what it shows.
.PP
The page can show only what carries some tags (the tag view).
The drop\-down above Act now lists every tag the page\(aqs items carry,
with how many things carry it; picking one, or clicking an item\(aqs
\f[CR]#tag\f[R] badge, adds it to the selection, and the page then shows
only the items carrying at least one of the tags selected: in Act now,
in every panel and in focus mode.
An item carries the tags its badges show.
On the page, one with parts (an entry\(aqs todos) is kept whole, parts
and all, when it or one of its parts carries one; focus mode, which
steps through a panel\(aqs parts one at a time, skips those carrying
none (unless their item does).
Act now shows the most urgent of what carries one, numbered from #1; an
item joining a ticket\(aqs move to its PR\(aqs step is shown whole when
either carries it, so its Snooze sets aside both, as unfiltered.
Panels and groups left empty are left off; a plugin whose group is left
off says when it last refreshed, or why it failed, and its problems, in
the footer, with its Refresh button.
While tags are selected, groups and panels can\(aqt be dragged.
Each selected tag has a link clearing it, beside one clearing them all,
and one that nothing carries any more stays there to clear.
The selection is in the page\(aqs address
(\f[CR]/?tag=chores&tag=garbage\-day\f[R]), so it can be bookmarked, and
the page\(aqs reload, focus mode\(aqs links and the buttons\(aq
redirects keep it.
.SH SETTINGS
\f[CR]/settings\f[R] (Settings, in the header) shows the config\(aqs
settings as a form: the top level\(aqs, \f[CR][act_now]\f[R]\(aqs,
\f[CR][notifications]\f[R]\(aq and each enabled plugin\(aqs, each with
what it does.
Saving writes only the settings that changed into the config file, each
on its own line, keeping the file\(aqs comments and hand edits, and only
if the result loads; otherwise the page says why and nothing is written.
\f[CR]mfd serve\f[R] then restarts with it, as it does whenever the file
changes.
Tables such as \f[CR][slack.teams]\f[R] are edited in the file, and
\f[CR]db_path\f[R] and \f[CR]port\f[R] aren\(aqt changed here, since
changing them moves the server.
Nor are the settings that say where a credential goes or comes from,
whom mail trusts, or where commands run: \f[CR]email\f[R],
\f[CR][github] host\f[R],
\f[CR][jira] site\f[R]/\f[CR]token_env\f[R]/\f[CR]keychain_service\f[R],
\f[CR][mail]\f[R]
\f[CR]host\f[R]/\f[CR]port\f[R]/\f[CR]token_env\f[R]/\f[CR]keychain_service\f[R]/\f[CR]message_url\f[R]/\f[CR]auth_servers\f[R],
\f[CR][slack]\f[R]
\f[CR]workspace_url\f[R]/\f[CR]token_env\f[R]/\f[CR]keychain_service\f[R]
and \f[CR][git]\f[R] \f[CR]repos\f[R]/\f[CR]github_hosts\f[R].
They\(aqre shown read\-only; edit the file.
.SH NOTIFICATIONS
The dashboard can tell you in the browser when something changes: a PR
becomes mergeable, a plugin can\(aqt fetch.
Nothing is on until you subscribe, by ticking kinds of events under
Notifications on \f[CR]/settings\f[R], answering
\f[CR]mfd init\f[R]\(aqs question, or listing them in
\f[CR][notifications] subscribe\f[R] (for example
\f[CR][\(dqdashboard.source_failing\(dq]\f[R]).
Press \(dqAllow in this browser\(dq on the settings page once.
.IP \(bu 2
Each plugin compares what it fetched, and what the others did, with what
they had last time (\f[CR]Collector.events\f[R]), and core compares the
plugins\(aq panels with the last refresh\(aqs for what\(aqs newly in
them (a branch joining Cleanup), so the first fetch never notifies, and
only a change does, after a restart too.
Events of kinds you haven\(aqt subscribed to are never stored.
.IP \(bu 2
Events wait on the server until a dashboard tab connects.
The server streams them to open tabs without a browser timer; older
browsers ask every 15 seconds instead.
Events from while no tab was open are told when you open one.
Events older than \f[CR]keep_hours\f[R] (24) are dropped.
.IP \(bu 2
Events about the same thing, say one PR\(aqs CI failing and a new push,
come as one notification, even if many events have piled up.
A request takes up to 20 different things, so repeats about one PR
cannot hold up other alerts.
Clicking one opens the dashboard at the item, which is highlighted.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]dashboard.source_failing\f[R]: a plugin has failed to fetch for
\f[CR]failing_minutes\f[R] (15), said once until it works again.
.SH FOCUS MODE
The Focus button in the header opens a list of what to walk through: Act
now, and each panel under its group\(aqs title, with counts.
Pick one to see a single item at a time, with Prev and Next (or the left
and right arrow keys).
Act now goes through its items in their order overall; a panel, in its
own, with the panel\(aqs own buttons (Delete all, New todo) above its
item.
Work entries are split into their todos (notes says how), each with the
entry\(aqs buttons that no todo has (Put on ice).
With tags selected (see Tags), it goes through only what carries one.
Buttons on a focused item come back to the same item afterwards,
wherever they moved it (a todo made quick comes first).
One that takes it out of the category (a todo ticked off, an item
snoozed) leaves it shown as the page has it now (the todo ticked,
\(dqdone\(dq; the item with Unsnooze, \(dqset aside\(dq), so what it did
can be undone, or, when the page no longer shows it at all, as it was,
without its buttons, \(dqgone\(dq.
A focus page\(aqs address names its item as \f[CR]kept\f[R]: its anchor
(\f[CR]item\-\&...\f[R]), or \f[CR]title:\f[R] and its title for an item
about nothing.
.SH API
Everything the page does goes through plain HTTP on
\f[CR]127.0.0.1:<port>\f[R], so a script (or Claude) can drive it.
Browsers use \f[CR]mfd.localhost:<port>\f[R] (browsers send every
\f[CR]*.localhost\f[R] name to this machine).
\f[CR]mfd dump\f[R] prints the same data as JSON.
.PP
Every request must carry the dashboard\(aqs token, so other programs and
users on the machine can\(aqt read the page or press its buttons, but
\f[CR]/login\f[R] and its handshake (\f[CR]/login/challenge\f[R],
\f[CR]/login/code\f[R]) and the static files.
The token is in \f[CR]token\f[R] next to the database
(\f[CR]\(ti/.local/share/move\-forward/token\f[R]), made on first start
and readable only by you.
Scripts send it as \f[CR]Authorization: Bearer <token>\f[R]; it never
goes in a URL.
Browsers never hold it: \f[CR]mfd open\f[R] signs the browser in,
through the handshake, with a session that lasts 400 days.
A request without the token or a session gets
\f[CR]401 Unauthorized\f[R].
\f[CR]mfd logout\f[R] or \f[CR]mfd token rotate\f[R] ends every session.
How the handshake and sessions work is in
\f[CR]src/move_forward/auth.py\f[R]\(aqs docstring.
.PP
Requests must also name the server as \f[CR]mfd.localhost:<port>\f[R],
\f[CR]127.0.0.1:<port>\f[R] or \f[CR]localhost:<port>\f[R] in their
\f[CR]Host\f[R] header, and a POST that carries an \f[CR]Origin\f[R]
header must come from one of those, so other web pages open in the
browser can\(aqt use the dashboard\(aqs buttons, while scripts like
\f[CR]curl\f[R], which send no \f[CR]Origin\f[R], work.
Anything else gets \f[CR]403 Forbidden\f[R].
A script with the token is served at whichever of them it asks; a page
asked for without it at \f[CR]127.0.0.1\f[R] or \f[CR]localhost\f[R] is
redirected to \f[CR]mfd.localhost\f[R], where the browser\(aqs session
is.
.PP
